<!--
  Custom Wazuh detection rule — targets log tampering / anti-forensics.

  Finding: the generic FIM rule 550 ("Integrity checksum changed") fires
  identically whether a monitored file grows, shrinks, or just changes
  permissions/ownership — it doesn't say WHICH attribute changed. For a
  log file like auth.log, a SIZE change is the specific signal worth
  calling out: a healthy, active auth.log should only ever grow, so any
  size change (especially a drop) is worth a dedicated, higher-severity
  alert rather than being lost in the generic 550 stream.

  Design (revised): an earlier version of this rule tried to approximate
  "before < after" with digit-count regexes on size_before/size_after
  (e.g. 3+ digits vs 1-2 digits). That approach needed hand-tuned
  thresholds and didn't account for Wazuh's own self-logging floor
  (sudo/PAM immediately writing a line back into the file it just
  watched being truncated, landing the "after" size well above 0).
  This version instead checks Wazuh's own `changed_fields` attribute
  list for "size" directly — if syscheck reports that the file's size
  was one of the attributes that changed, the rule fires, regardless of
  the exact byte values involved. This is simpler and more robust than
  guessing a threshold.

  Trade-off / known limitation: this rule fires on ANY size change to
  auth.log, growth included — not just shrinkage. A single truncation
  and the routine follow-up write it causes (self-logging) will both
  show up as separate 100101 alerts. The alert is an investigation
  signal ("auth.log's size just changed — go look why"), not standalone
  proof of tampering. Correlating it with surrounding PAM/sudo events
  (as done in writeup #4) is what turns it into an actual verdict.

  Prerequisite: /var/log must be added to the agent's <syscheck>
  directories (realtime="yes") and the default `.log$` ignore pattern in
  <ignore type="sregex"> narrowed, otherwise log files are never watched
  by FIM at all — auth.log is excluded from monitoring by default.

  Install: place in /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml on the MANAGER,
  then `sudo systemctl restart wazuh-manager`.

  Status: deployed and confirmed firing live. Verified against a real
  controlled truncation of /var/log/auth.log (1502 -> 81 bytes) and the
  subsequent routine self-logging growth (81 -> 386 bytes) — both
  transitions triggered this rule, which also demonstrates the
  "fires on growth too" limitation noted above.
-->
<rule id="100101" level="12">
  <if_sid>550</if_sid>
  <field name="file">^/var/log/auth\.log$</field>
  <field name="changed_fields">size</field>
  <description>auth.log size changed - investigate possible log tampering</description>
  <mitre>
    <id>T1070.002</id>
  </mitre>
  <group>log_tampering,defense_evasion,</group>
</rule>
